        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "scalpel_blade_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction and identification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "scalpel_blade_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A scalpel blade is a sharp, usually single-use surgical cutting component that is either attached to a compatible scalpel handle or supplied as part of a disposable scalpel."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Category: Cutting and dissecting instrument component.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Primary function: To incise skin and other tissues with precision.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Material: Commonly stainless steel or carbon steel; specialist blades may use other materials.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Sterility: Supplied sterile for surgical use.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Typical design: A sharpened cutting edge, a tip or point where applicable, and a mounting portion that attaches to a compatible handle.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "scalpel_blade_group_3",
              "group_title": "How to identify it in an OSCE",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Look for a small, thin metallic blade with a sharpened edge and a characteristic shape. The shape helps you identify its number and likely use."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Model identification statement:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is a surgical scalpel blade, a cutting component used with a compatible scalpel handle to make precise surgical incisions. It is available in different sizes and shapes for different procedures.”",
                  "style": "quote"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "scalpel_blade_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Labelling of the scalpel blade",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "scalpel_blade_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/scalpel_blade_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "surgicalwale.com"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The important parts are:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/scalpel_blade_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. Tip or point"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The terminal end of the blade. It may be sharp and pointed or have a different profile depending on the blade design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/scalpel_blade_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Cutting edge"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The sharpened portion that divides tissue."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/scalpel_blade_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3. Spine or back"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The non-cutting edge opposite the cutting edge in many conventional blade designs."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/scalpel_blade_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "4. Mounting portion"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The proximal part that engages with a compatible scalpel handle."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Note: Exact blade anatomy varies. Some blade designs have more than one cutting edge, and not all have a clearly identifiable point or conventional mounting slot."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "scalpel_blade_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Classification and types of scalpel blades",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "scalpel_blade_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The most important OSCE classification is by blade number."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/scalpel_blade_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. 10 blade"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Broad, curved cutting edge.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Used for general skin incisions.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Common in laparotomy, hernia repair and other open operations.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/scalpel_blade_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. 11 blade"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Elongated triangular blade with a pointed tip.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Used for small stab incisions and selected drainage or access procedures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Its sharp point demands particular care.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/scalpel_blade_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. 12 blade"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Hook-shaped blade.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Used in selected ENT, dental and other procedures where its curved profile is advantageous.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/scalpel_blade_12.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. 15 blade"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Small blade with a curved cutting edge.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Used for short, precise incisions and minor surgical procedures, including selected skin excisions.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/scalpel_blade_13.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. 20 blade"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Large, broad blade in the No. 10 family of shapes.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Used for larger incisions where a broad cutting edge is appropriate.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/scalpel_blade_14.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. 22 blade"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Large blade with a curved cutting edge.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Used for selected long or large incisions.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These are common blade patterns; the specific choice depends on the procedure, the surgeon's preference and the manufacturer's specifications."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "scalpel_blade_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Scalpel blade and handle compatibility",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "scalpel_blade_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is a frequent viva question."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/scalpel_blade_15.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/scalpel_blade_16.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Handle family",
                    "Common compatible blades"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "No. 3 handle",
                      "Nos. 10, 11, 12 and 15"
                    ],
                    [
                      "No. 4 handle",
                      "Nos. 20, 21, 22, 23 and 24"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important: This is a common compatibility guide, not a universal rule for every manufacturer or handle variant. Check the actual fitting and instructions for use."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "scalpel_blade_group_7",
              "group_title": "Common examiner questions",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q. Which handle is commonly used with a No. 15 blade?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. A No. 3 scalpel handle."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q. Which handle commonly accommodates a No. 22 blade?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. A No. 4 scalpel handle."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q. Why must compatibility be checked?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. An incompatible blade may not engage securely, creating a risk of loosening, slipping or injury during surgery."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "scalpel_blade_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Principles of blade selection and use",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "scalpel_blade_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "The correct blade is chosen according to:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Length of incision: A larger blade may be appropriate for a long incision.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Required precision: A smaller blade may be preferred for short, delicate incisions.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Blade profile: Pointed, curved or hooked designs suit different cutting requirements.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Tissue and procedure: The approach must suit the anatomical site and operation.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Handle compatibility: The blade must fit and lock securely onto the handle.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Sterility and integrity: The blade must be sterile, intact and suitable for the intended procedure.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The goal is to produce a controlled incision while minimising unnecessary tissue damage."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "scalpel_blade_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Advantages and disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "scalpel_blade_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Advantages"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Sharp and precise cutting edge.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Different shapes for different surgical requirements.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Usually inexpensive and widely available.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Sterile, single-use blades reduce the need to reprocess the cutting component.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Compatible reusable handles reduce instrument replacement costs.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Disadvantages"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Can cause serious cuts and puncture injuries.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Does not provide haemostasis.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May become blunt, damaged or contaminated.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Incorrect mounting can cause the blade to detach.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Requires safe handling, removal and sharps disposal.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "scalpel_blade_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "scalpel_blade_group_10",
              "group_title": "Patient-related",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Haemorrhage due to vessel injury.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Injury to nerves, tendons, vessels or deeper structures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Wound contamination and infection.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Excessive tissue trauma and poor wound healing.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Unintended deep incision or damage to adjacent structures.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "scalpel_blade_group_11",
              "group_title": "Operator-related",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Accidental cuts during handling.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Injury while mounting or removing the blade.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Sharps injury with potential exposure to blood-borne pathogens.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Injury caused by an insecurely mounted or damaged blade.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "scalpel_blade_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Safe mounting, removal and disposal",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "scalpel_blade_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/scalpel_blade_17.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/scalpel_blade_18.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For an OSCE, explain the safety principles clearly. Actual blade mounting and removal should be demonstrated only using the approved device and the institution's trained technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Verify the correct blade and compatible handle.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Maintain aseptic technique.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use an approved blade-mounting method; avoid direct contact with the cutting edge.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Check that the blade is securely engaged before use.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid passing a scalpel directly hand-to-hand when a neutral-zone technique is appropriate.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Remove detachable blades using an approved blade remover or other approved safety system.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Discard used blades immediately into an approved sharps container.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Do not reuse a single-use blade or place it in ordinary waste.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "If a sharps injury occurs, stop the activity and follow the institution's exposure-management protocol promptly."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "scalpel_blade_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "scalpel_blade_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is the difference between a scalpel and a scalpel blade?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A scalpel is the complete cutting instrument; a scalpel blade is its cutting component."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. Which blade is used for general skin incisions?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. 10 is a common choice."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Which blade is associated with a pointed triangular shape?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. 11."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Which blade is useful for fine, short incisions?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. 15."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. Which blade has a hook-shaped profile?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. 12."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Does a scalpel blade coagulate blood vessels?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. It cuts tissue but does not provide coagulation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. What is the danger of an incorrectly mounted blade?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It may loosen or detach during surgery, causing injury or interrupting the procedure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. How should a used blade be discarded?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Into an approved sharps container using the designated safe disposal method."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "scalpel_blade_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Quick revision card",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "scalpel_blade_group_14",
              "group_title": "Remember the blade numbers",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "10 — General incision"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "11 — Pointed blade, stab incision"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "12 — Hook-shaped blade"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "15 — Small, precise incision"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "20–24 — Larger blade family, commonly associated with No. 4 handles"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Three safety priorities: Correct compatibility, secure mounting, safe sharps disposal."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "**Model 30-second OSCE presentation**",
                  "style": "heading"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is a surgical scalpel blade, a cutting instrument component used with a compatible handle to make precise incisions. Common types include No. 10 for general skin incisions, No. 11 for stab incisions, No. 12 for selected hooked cuts and No. 15 for fine incisions. The blade must be sterile, intact and securely mounted. Complications include haemorrhage, injury to adjacent structures, wound infection and sharps injuries. A used blade must be disposed of in an approved sharps container.”",
                  "style": "quote"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}